You get two inputs: (1) a receipt IMAGE and (2) an OCR TEXT draft.
Use OCR as a hint, but the IMAGE is the source of truth. Fix OCR errors by checking the image.

Output ONLY:
| # | Item | Price |
|---:|---|---:|
...rows...
NUMBER_OF_ITEMS: <int>
ITEM_COUNT: <int>

Rules:
1) One printed receipt line = one row. Never merge lines.
2) Copy Item text verbatim from that line (no normalization).
3) Lines starting with “+” (e.g., +CRV …) must be their own rows.
4) Price comes ONLY from the same line’s right side; never borrow from nearby lines.
5) Price format: digits only, exactly 2 decimals; strip flags like F/T; “.05” -> “0.05”.
6) If price is unreadable/missing, leave Price blank (don’t guess).
7) Keep headers/totals only if they are their own printed lines; ignore address/date/card/tender/etc.
8) Use OCR to propose lines, then:
   - correct characters and decimals,
   - split any OCR run-on lines into separate printed lines,
   - drop OCR lines not on the image,
   - add image lines missing from OCR.
9) Read NUMBER OF ITEMS from the image.
10) Compute ITEM_COUNT = count of rows with non-blank Price excluding totals (SUBTOTAL/TAX/TOTAL); include CRV lines if priced.
11) If ITEM_COUNT ≠ NUMBER_OF_ITEMS, re-check for missed prices; if still off, keep best-faith values with no extra commentary.
